// =============================================================================
// SERVERLESS → ON-PREMISES ON AZURE WITHOUT PNG: APPLICATION GATEWAY v2 (MANAGED REVERSE PROXY)
//
// JSONC for you to read; emit PLAIN JSON (strip the // lines) when you write
// architecture.md. Read reference/style-networking.md first.
//
// HOW IT WORKS ON DATABRICKS (use this to adapt the diagram to the user's network):
//   • An alternative when a Private Network Gateway (PNG) is not an option. Serverless
//     reaches the customer network through a PRIVATE ENDPOINT RULE in the workspace's
//     Network Connectivity Configuration (NCC), here pointing at an Azure Application
//     Gateway v2 instead of a Private Link Service.
//   • The customer creates two subnets in the hub VNet: one for the Application Gateway
//     and one for its Private Link Configuration (the private IP addresses the private
//     endpoint connects to). The gateway needs a standard public IP and frontend IP type
//     "Both" (private-only does not support Private Link); bind the listener to the
//     PRIVATE frontend and leave the public one unused. Then configure listeners,
//     routing rules and backend pools; backends can be private IPs or FQDNs, including
//     on-premises over the hub's ExpressRoute / S2S VPN gateway.
//   • What it gives: a managed, terminating reverse proxy, no proxy VMs. Layer 7 for
//     HTTP(S) / WebSockets (path- and host-header routing, cookie affinity) and Layer 4
//     TCP/TLS proxying (Public Preview) for targets like Postgres or MySQL.
//   • Watch out: NSGs on the App Gateway subnet must allow inbound from App Gateway
//     Manager and Azure Load Balancer (optionally deny internet inbound at a lower
//     priority). The Layer 4 proxy is itself in Public Preview.
//   • Choose it when the targets are HTTP(S) and/or TCP and the customer wants a managed
//     service. For ultra-low latency / high throughput or existing proxy VMs, use the
//     Private Link Service + load balancer (reference/serverless-pls-azure.jsonc); if
//     on-prem forbids inbound connections from the cloud, use the reverse SSH tunnel
//     (reference/serverless-tunnel-azure.jsonc).
//
// WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES (layout):
//   • The top level runs in traffic order: serverless → private endpoint rule → Private Link
//     Configuration IPs → Application Gateway → VPN / ExpressRoute gateway → link →
//     on-premises. Each hop is one subnet box or one tile, and each carries `track: "t1"`,
//     so the red path is a straight line.
//   • Same skeleton as the other serverless-to-on-prem references, so the reader sees
//     what changes between options: only the hub's middle hops.
//   • The workspace VNet carries no serverless traffic: one card, dashed peering.
//
// ADAPT IT: name the actual backends (an HTTP API, a Postgres host) in place of the
// generic on-prem trio when the user gives them; mark Layer 4 targets as Public Preview.
// =============================================================================
